; core/secure-channel: an end-to-end authenticated, encrypted channel between
; two devices that can only reach each other through a relay. transport.cddl
; describes a relay as an opaque, unreadable byte pipe whose `relay-data`
; payload is "ciphertext established one layer above the transport"; this is
; that layer. Without it a hub, and anything able to read its traffic, sees
; every manage-request and manage-response a pairing carries, can alter them
; (WebRTC signalling included, DTLS fingerprint and all), and a receiver has
; only the hub's own `from-device` to say who sent a request, which room.cddl
; forbids trusting.
;
; A channel lives between exactly two devices and inside one relay pairing. It
; is set up by an exchange of `secure-hello-frame`s and then carries every
; manage-request, manage-response and core/data frame (data-have, data-request
; and data-entries) in `secure-data-frame`s, both of which travel as the
; payload of `relay-data-frame`. Carrying core/data frames is what lets two
; devices that reach each other only through a relay replicate logs between
; themselves; a relay's own mailbox (discovery.cddl) is addressed directly,
; never through a channel. The relay sees the two hellos in
; the clear (an ephemeral public key, a nonce and an identity key, none of
; which is secret) and everything after as ciphertext.
;
; Handshake. Each side generates a fresh ECDH P-256 key pair and a random
; 16-byte nonce for every channel, and sends a hello carrying the public key,
; the nonce, its own identity-key and a signature made with that identity key
; over
;
;   "wire-mesh/secure-hello/v1" 0x00 || ephemeral-key || nonce ||
;   sender-device || recipient-device
;
; where sender-device is the device-id derived from the hello's own
; identity-key and recipient-device is the hello's `to-device`. Every part has
; a fixed length, so the concatenation is unambiguous. Binding both device-ids
; means a hello cannot be replayed to a different recipient or attributed to a
; different sender, and a relay that substitutes an ephemeral key invalidates
; the signature. Either side may send first. A side that receives a hello
; while it has none outstanding for that peer answers with its own; a side that
; has one outstanding treats the received hello as the answer. Two sides that
; open at once therefore agree without either one answering the other.
;
; Key schedule. Let lo and hi be the two device-ids in bytewise order and
; nonce-lo and nonce-hi the nonces carried by the hellos of the devices those
; ids name. The shared secret is the 32-byte ECDH result of the two ephemeral
; keys. Two 32-byte AES-256-GCM keys, one per direction, come from HKDF-SHA256
; with that secret as input keying material, nonce-lo || nonce-hi as salt, and
;
;   "wire-mesh/secure-channel/v1" 0x00 || lo || hi || label
;
; as info, where label is the ASCII string "lo-to-hi" for the key that seals
; frames sent by lo, and "hi-to-lo" for the one sealing frames sent by hi.
;
; Data. Each direction keeps a counter that starts at 0 and rises by one per
; frame. A frame's plaintext is the canonical CBOR encoding of one inner Frame.
; It is sealed with AES-256-GCM under the sender's directional key, with a
; 96-bit IV of four zero bytes followed by the counter as a big-endian uint64,
; and associated data
;
;   "wire-mesh/secure-data/v1" 0x00 || sender-device || recipient-device ||
;   counter (big-endian uint64)
;
; Verifier obligations, none of which the grammar can express:
;
; 1. A hello MUST be refused unless its identity-key hashes to a device-id
;    (self-certification, as everywhere else), its signature verifies under
;    that key over the input above, and its `to-device` is the receiver's own
;    device-id.
; 2. The identity a request is attributed to is the one a hello authenticated,
;    never the `from-device` a relay stamps on `relay-data`. That field only
;    says which channel to try.
; 3. A manage-request, manage-response or core/data frame that arrives as the
;    payload of a `relay-data-frame` without being sealed in a
;    `secure-data-frame` MUST be dropped. There is no plaintext fallback, since one would let a relay
;    strip the protection.
; 4. A `secure-data-frame` MUST be dropped unless it opens under the channel
;    for that peer and its counter is greater than every counter already
;    accepted from that peer on that channel.
; 5. Only a manage-request, manage-response, data-have, data-request or
;    data-entries frame may be sealed inside a `secure-data-frame`; anything
;    else MUST be dropped after opening. A core/data frame is attributed to the
;    device the channel authenticated, never to the log its `peer` field names.
; 6. Ephemeral keys and nonces are fresh for every channel and MUST NOT be
;    reused. A channel does not outlive the relay connection it was set up on.
;
; Availability against the relay is not a goal: it can drop, delay or replay
; frames, and a replayed old hello can only make a channel unusable, never
; readable or forgeable.

secure-hello-frame = {
  type: "secure-hello",
  "ephemeral-key": bstr .size 65,   ; SEC1 uncompressed P-256 point: 0x04 || X || Y
  nonce: bstr .size 16,
  "identity-key": identity-key,
  "to-device": device-id,
  signature: bstr,                  ; over the hello signing input above, alg per identity-key
}

secure-data-frame = {
  type: "secure-data",
  counter: uint,
  ciphertext: bstr,                 ; AES-256-GCM output: ciphertext followed by the 16-byte tag
}

$frame-variant /= secure-hello-frame
$frame-variant /= secure-data-frame
